WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.164 --> 00:00:02.035
-Cecily!

00:00:02.035 --> 00:00:03.369
-Sethily!

00:00:03.369 --> 00:00:05.538
-It is so lovely
to have you back.

00:00:05.538 --> 00:00:07.006
-Great to be back.

00:00:07.006 --> 00:00:10.176
-You, like a lot of us, have
had major changes this year.

00:00:10.176 --> 00:00:12.345
You were --
you sort of decamped.

00:00:12.345 --> 00:00:14.347
You moved up
to Upstate New York.

00:00:14.347 --> 00:00:15.714
-Yes, Hudson valley.

00:00:15.714 --> 00:00:18.551
-How is rural life suiting you?

00:00:18.551 --> 00:00:21.888
-I think I'm becoming
a real mountain woman.

00:00:21.888 --> 00:00:23.723
I'm getting there.

00:00:23.723 --> 00:00:27.026
I did just fall
and hurt myself, though.

00:00:27.026 --> 00:00:30.196
My dog is actually more of
a mountain woman than I am

00:00:30.196 --> 00:00:34.533
and she's taken it on herself
to hunt the woodchucks.

00:00:34.533 --> 00:00:35.768
-Okay.
-Yeah.

00:00:35.768 --> 00:00:37.436
And I --
-Not a big dog, your dog.

00:00:37.436 --> 00:00:39.605
-Not a big dog.
-Okay.

00:00:39.605 --> 00:00:41.440
-And not a big woodchuck.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:42.775
-Okay, so it's fair.

00:00:42.775 --> 00:00:45.945
-Yeah, it's a fair -- So,
she just fought a woodchuck.

00:00:45.945 --> 00:00:47.279
-And how did you get hurt?

00:00:47.279 --> 00:00:49.515
-So, I had just gotten
out of the shower.

00:00:49.515 --> 00:00:51.017
This has nothing to do with it

00:00:51.017 --> 00:00:53.519
except for that I threw on,
like, a housedress.

00:00:53.519 --> 00:00:56.021
I was just gonna take her out
to pee quickly.

00:00:56.021 --> 00:00:57.690
Wasn't wearing underwear.

00:00:57.690 --> 00:00:59.859
And then I saw that Lucy --

00:00:59.859 --> 00:01:02.028
I saw a woodchuck kind of leap,

00:01:02.028 --> 00:01:04.864
and I knew my dog was in a fight
with a woodchuck.

00:01:04.864 --> 00:01:08.534
So I ran.
I fell down the hill.

00:01:08.534 --> 00:01:10.336
Gave them all a real view.

00:01:10.336 --> 00:01:11.904
-Yeah.
-All the woodchucks.

00:01:11.904 --> 00:01:13.239
And then I had to...

00:01:13.239 --> 00:01:15.574
Like, I can't see my dog
kill a woodchuck,

00:01:15.574 --> 00:01:17.576
and I can't see a woodchuck
kill my dog.

00:01:17.576 --> 00:01:18.911
And it was very dramatic.

00:01:18.911 --> 00:01:22.749
I was like, "Lucy, no!
Please! Kevin!"

00:01:22.749 --> 00:01:25.751
And then I finally
yanked her up,

00:01:25.751 --> 00:01:27.921
and Kevin, who I live with now,

00:01:27.921 --> 00:01:29.923
came outside and was like,
"Are you okay?"

00:01:29.923 --> 00:01:32.658
And I was holding Lucy and I
truly went...

00:01:32.658 --> 00:01:34.761
[dramatically] "No, I'm not."

00:01:34.761 --> 00:01:36.095
[ Laughter ]

00:01:36.095 --> 00:01:37.630
-I'm so glad
you added that detail

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:39.298
about Kevin being
someone you live with

00:01:39.298 --> 00:01:40.966
'cause I thought you had
named the woodchuck.

00:01:40.966 --> 00:01:41.967
-Oh, no.
-That, like...

00:01:41.967 --> 00:01:43.169
-I wouldn't choose --

00:01:43.169 --> 00:01:45.672
Well, Kevin is a good
woodchuck name, actually.

00:01:45.672 --> 00:01:47.506
-I also feel like this is...

00:01:47.506 --> 00:01:49.175
People who live in rural areas,

00:01:49.175 --> 00:01:52.512
when they fear people from
cities moving there --

00:01:52.512 --> 00:01:54.547
everything you did
is what they fear.

00:01:54.547 --> 00:01:56.215
-Yeah,
but they're probably like,

00:01:56.215 --> 00:01:59.552
whatever she's doing wrong is
all the woodchucks are there,

00:01:59.552 --> 00:02:00.920
and then they're not there.

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:03.089
And then, like, the woodchuck
ran away and I truly --

00:02:03.089 --> 00:02:04.790
then it was all like you could
hear them like --

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:06.125
[ Chittering ]

00:02:06.125 --> 00:02:08.127
Like, "What happened to you?!
Are you okay?!"

00:02:08.127 --> 00:02:10.930
[ Laughter ]

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:12.765
-That's great,
that they want to, you know --

00:02:12.765 --> 00:02:14.500
I mean, if something interesting
happens to a woodchuck,

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:15.835
you want to tell
the other woodchucks.

00:02:15.835 --> 00:02:18.170
-Right, I'm glad they took care
of their friend.

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:19.539
-I didn't know...

00:02:19.539 --> 00:02:21.874
Again, I've never heard
an impression of a woodchuck.

00:02:21.874 --> 00:02:23.876
That was excellent.
But I also heard --

00:02:23.876 --> 00:02:25.211
I didn't know deers --

00:02:25.211 --> 00:02:27.146
I've seen a lot of deers
this past year --

00:02:27.146 --> 00:02:28.814
-Is it deers?

00:02:28.814 --> 00:02:29.815
-Deer. You're right.

00:02:29.815 --> 00:02:30.817
-Okay.
-Yeah.

00:02:30.817 --> 00:02:32.451
[ Laughter ]

00:02:32.451 --> 00:02:33.619
Boy.

00:02:33.619 --> 00:02:36.789
Now it's happening
in real time.

00:02:36.789 --> 00:02:39.292
-So you're a real mountain guy,
too, yeah.

00:02:39.292 --> 00:02:42.295
-I think if you -- if they are
beloved to you --

00:02:42.295 --> 00:02:44.129
-They are your deers.
-Your dears, yes.

00:02:44.129 --> 00:02:46.132
-Yes.
-Yes, it's like...

00:02:46.132 --> 00:02:47.967
Okay, sure, they're also deer...

00:02:47.967 --> 00:02:49.635
-Okay, yeah, yeah.
-But my dears.

00:02:49.635 --> 00:02:50.970
-Yes. Alright.

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:54.307
-But have you -- you've had
a deer scream at you?

00:02:54.307 --> 00:02:55.975
-Yes, last summer.

00:02:55.975 --> 00:02:57.810
And I didn't know it was a deer.

00:02:57.810 --> 00:02:59.479
I didn't know deer scream.

00:02:59.479 --> 00:03:00.646
-I didn't know that either.

00:03:00.646 --> 00:03:03.983
-And I was outside again
with my dog, in a dress.

00:03:03.983 --> 00:03:06.652
I was wearing underwear
this time, and...

00:03:06.652 --> 00:03:08.922
And I just heard this like --

00:03:08.922 --> 00:03:10.756
"Cawww!" or something.
-[ Laughing ]

00:03:10.756 --> 00:03:13.126
-I can't --
it's such a weird sound.

00:03:13.126 --> 00:03:15.128
I had to go try
to, like, google it.

00:03:15.128 --> 00:03:17.797
And I kept looking around like,
"What is that?"

00:03:17.797 --> 00:03:20.967
And then finally, like --
"Haaah!"

00:03:20.967 --> 00:03:25.238
And I looked again and there was
this, like, deer staring at me.

00:03:25.238 --> 00:03:28.074
And I was, like,
for the first time in my life,

00:03:28.074 --> 00:03:29.742
afraid of a deer.
-Yeah.

00:03:29.742 --> 00:03:31.577
-Of deers. Of my dears.
-[ Laughing ]

00:03:31.577 --> 00:03:33.413
-They were not my dears
in that moment.

00:03:33.413 --> 00:03:34.914
-They were no longer
your dears then, yeah.

00:03:34.914 --> 00:03:36.082
-And we went inside.

00:03:36.082 --> 00:03:37.750
But I feel like
this has also been...

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:41.520
I have googled
so many animal sounds

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:42.855
for like my last --

00:03:42.855 --> 00:03:45.591
the last year and a half of my
google searches is like,

00:03:45.591 --> 00:03:47.593
"What sound"

00:03:47.593 --> 00:03:51.764
or, "What animal makes a sound
like a cat but angry

00:03:51.764 --> 00:03:54.200
in the middle of the night,
Hudson Valley, New York?"

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:55.701
[ Laughter ]

00:03:55.701 --> 00:03:59.372
I have, like, a bird --
I have two bird sound apps.

00:03:59.372 --> 00:04:00.673
-Really?
-Yeah.

00:04:00.673 --> 00:04:03.376
Being like, "What goes like
da-da-da-da-da-da-da?"

00:04:03.376 --> 00:04:05.878
and then trying to...
but that's hard to google.

00:04:05.878 --> 00:04:07.279
-Yeah, 'cause what do you --

00:04:07.279 --> 00:04:10.115
Do you make it into the app
or do you wait for the bird?

00:04:10.115 --> 00:04:12.952
-I've tried to, like,
phonetically try to write out

00:04:12.952 --> 00:04:14.787
the sounds that I'm hearing.

00:04:14.787 --> 00:04:16.521
-Okay.
-But people do.

00:04:16.521 --> 00:04:18.191
-How often have you...

00:04:18.191 --> 00:04:20.493
Have you ever had a hit
from the app

00:04:20.493 --> 00:04:22.161
telling you what kind
of bird it was?

00:04:22.161 --> 00:04:25.498
-I've, at least, assumed that it
was right,

00:04:25.498 --> 00:04:28.167
that I was identify--
-Don't take this the wrong way.

00:04:28.167 --> 00:04:30.002
Like, based on the noises you're
making,

00:04:30.002 --> 00:04:32.572
I feel like the best it could do
was either like a binary

00:04:32.572 --> 00:04:34.607
"Yes, that's a bird"
or "No, it's not a bird."

00:04:34.607 --> 00:04:37.109
-Well, I think I know...

00:04:37.109 --> 00:04:40.446
It's that whatever goes like,
"Da-da-da-da-da-da!"

00:04:40.446 --> 00:04:43.616
Whatever that is, is like
a starling or a robin, I think.

00:04:43.616 --> 00:04:45.450
-Okay, that's great.
-Yeah.

00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:47.654
-We -- I did --

00:04:47.654 --> 00:04:51.156
Well, like, we had a game that
anytime a bird made a noise,

00:04:51.156 --> 00:04:53.659
I would tell my boys that was
the bird saying hello.

00:04:53.659 --> 00:04:55.161
And so then
they would yell back.

00:04:55.161 --> 00:04:57.330
So it would be like that noise
and then be like, "Hi, bird!"

00:04:57.330 --> 00:04:58.831
And then the bird -- you know,
because the bird wasn't

00:04:58.831 --> 00:05:01.033
having a conversation --
would just say it again.

00:05:01.033 --> 00:05:02.968
And then the kids would...
So then I had to tell them,

00:05:02.968 --> 00:05:04.971
"You have to -- you can stop
saying hello to all the birds,"

00:05:04.971 --> 00:05:06.638
'cause it backfired
pretty quickly.

00:05:06.638 --> 00:05:07.940
-Just too much?

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:09.909
-Yeah, my kids, they were
saying hi to all the birds,

00:05:09.909 --> 00:05:12.378
and I just -- I forgot that
the birds would keep talking.

00:05:12.378 --> 00:05:13.579
"You don't have to keep
doing this."

00:05:13.579 --> 00:05:15.914
-Yeah, they'll respond.
-They will respond.

00:05:15.914 --> 00:05:17.616
I want to talk about
your wonderful book,

00:05:17.616 --> 00:05:18.951
and we're gonna do that.

00:05:18.951 --> 00:05:20.720
We'll be right back with more
from Cecily Strong.

